{"parentUuid":"ee8407eb-4e45-4b90-8f13-b76eae6eabc8","isSidechain":false,"type":"system","subtype":"away_summary","content":"Adding Codex PostToolUse support to fix the carry-forward bug; just landed the fix for the unlink-while-locked race in ClearSessionState. Next: wait for the next reviewer pass on the branch.","timestamp":"2026-05-08T09:22:45.251Z","uuid":"e78de91e-6751-44e4-b952-53779452bfdf","isMeta":false,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli","sessionId":"b1ed7d58-eb6b-498c-931f-1f6f207e13c3","version":"2.1.129","gitBranch":"soph/add-codex-post-tool"}
{"parentUuid":"e78de91e-6751-44e4-b952-53779452bfdf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4a1b0b81-c6be-459f-a65d-b817254b098a","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"can you review these and give me a weighted overview what we should do and what is easy to fix?\n\n  1. goroutineID() via runtime stack-string parsing (session_state.go:392) works and is a known idiom, but it's called on every MutateSessionState entry —\n  including the hot Codex PostToolUse path. The cost is a 64-byte stack snapshot + a couple of string scans, which should be negligible, but worth noting if\n  perf becomes a concern. The doc comment correctly disclaims it as a reentrancy key only — good.\n  2. sessionMutationGate sync.Map grows unbounded per process. Fine in practice because hook invocations are short-lived subprocesses, but if anything ever\n  turns into a longer-running daemon (entire status watcher, MCP server, etc.) this becomes a leak. Worth a one-liner doc note.\n  3. migrateAndPersistIfNeeded (manual_commit_migration.go:162) is now production-dead code — only manual_commit_test.go calls it. It does an unlocked\n  saveSessionState which would re-introduce the bug class you just fixed if a future caller picked it back up. I'd either delete it or convert it to use\n  MutateSessionState so it stays safe-by-construction.\n  4. postCommitProcessSession now relies on the outer MutateSessionState to persist (the inline saveSessionState got removed and replaced by // State is saved\n  by the outer MutateSessionState in PostCommit.). That comment is accurate, but the function takes state *SessionState and is large enough that a future\n  caller could plausibly invoke it standalone and silently lose mutations. Consider a stronger contract — e.g., a // MUST be called from inside\n  MutateSessionState; mutations are persisted by the caller. doc plus, ideally, state re-typed as something that documents the invariant (or assert via the\n  gate that we currently hold a lock).\n  5. Holding the per-session flock for fn's entire duration is correct, but fn inside PostCommit includes condensation (CondenseSession, shadow branch tree\n  builds, etc.) which can be slow. A concurrent PostToolUse on the same session will now block for the whole commit. This is the right tradeoff (we don't want\n  PostToolUse overwriting state mid-condense), but it does mean a slow commit can stall hook responses. Probably fine; just be aware if anyone reports a\n  slow-feeling agent during commit.\n  6. adoptReviewEnv in lifecycle.go:396: the diff check (state.Kind == before.Kind && state.ReviewPrompt == before.ReviewPrompt &&\n  slices.Equal(state.ReviewSkills, before.ReviewSkills)) makes a copy before := *state then compares fields. ReviewSkills is a slice — before.ReviewSkills\n  aliases the underlying array. If adoptReviewEnv ever mutated the slice in place (it currently assigns a fresh slice, so OK), the comparison would be wrong.\n  Not a bug today, but a footgun if the function evolves. A defensive slices.Clone(state.ReviewSkills) before the call would be cheaper insurance.\n  7. TestRecordFilesTouched_ParallelMergesAreSerialized uses goroutines but each goroutine has its own goroutine ID, so the gate's reentrancy path isn't\n  exercised — this test only proves the flock works. The reentrancy is covered separately by TestMutateSessionState_NestedCallsAreReentrant. That's fine, just\n  noting they're complementary, not redundant.\n  8. isApplyPatchTool (codex/lifecycle.go:148) correctly accepts the Write/Edit aliases. If the underlying Codex matcher ever adds more aliases, the test loop\n  in TestParseHookEvent_PostToolUse_AcceptsClaudeAliases is the right place to catch drift — link the test comment to codex-rs/core/src/tools/hook_names.rs\n  (which it already does)."},"uuid":"c517df10-44a0-4af4-b948-9b6072d9edcd","timestamp":"2026-05-08T09:32:29.889Z","permissionMode":"acceptEdits","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli"