{"parentUuid":"6f440ac9-bc45-46ec-b169-28b5205396b4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e382d8ba-c487-46bb-b52c-454e192fbc4f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"one last review: \n\n  1.  goroutineID()  can falsely report reentrancy on parse failure (low-risk\n  edge case).  session_state.go:397-415  returns  0  if the runtime stack\n  header doesn't parse. Combined with  acquireSessionGate  at line 494: when\n   gate.owner == 0  (initial state, or just released) and  gid == 0 , the\n  comparison succeeds and we take the reentrant branch despite no outer call\n  holding the lock. Result:  gate.activeState == nil  → caller sees\n   ErrStateNotFound  even though the state file exists, and the flock is never\n  acquired so the \"mutation\" runs unprotected if the caller doesn't surface\n  the error.\n  The realistic blast radius is tiny —  runtime.Stack  is well-behaved — but a\n  one-line guard removes the footgun:\n\n    gid := goroutineID()\n    if gid == 0 {\n        // Treat unparseable stacks as never-reentrant; better to deadlock-\n  prone\n        // serialize than to silently bypass the flock.\n        gid = -1 // sentinel that gate.owner can never legitimately equal\n    }\n\n  2. Stale/misleading comment in  transitionSessionTurnEnd \n  ( lifecycle.go:1002-1006 ). It says \"HandleTurnEnd may itself call into\n  session-state APIs; today it uses  s.saveSessionState  internally, which is\n  being migrated to MutateSessionState.\" I checked —  HandleTurnEnd \n  ( manual_commit_hooks.go:2594 ) doesn't call  saveSessionState  anywhere; it\n  mutates the state pointer in-place and lets the outer save persist.\n  Production code no longer has any non-test callers of  s.saveSessionState .\n  Either delete the comment or rewrite it to describe the actual contract:\n  \"HandleTurnEnd mutates state in-place; the outer MutateSessionState save\n  flushes it. Reentrant MutateSessionState calls within HandleTurnEnd reuse\n  this state pointer via the gate.\"\n 