{"parentUuid":"5ff70f11-5680-47a7-b390-66ee4edd918a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01FWLtt22FoZk2jZmGZjcJx6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here's what we did — it's a post-install hint in `cmd/entire/cli/setup.go`, not a \"welcome message\" per se.\n\n**Where:** After `entire enable --agent codex` reports `Installed N hooks for Codex`, only when `installedHooks > 0` (so re-runs that no-op stay quiet) and only when the agent is Codex.\n\n**What gets printed:**\n```\n✓ Installed N hooks for Codex\n    Codex 0.129+ requires you to approve new hooks once: run /hooks inside Codex to trust them.\n```\n\n**Why we did it that way:**\n\nThe alternative was pre-trusting on install — replicating Codex's `command_hook_hash` in Go and writing `trusted_hash` entries to the user's `~/.codex/config.toml`. We rejected that because:\n\n1. **Fragile.** The hash is over a canonical TOML→JSON serialization with very specific rules (sorted keys, omit-when-nil, no HTML escaping). Any drift in Codex's normalization silently breaks pre-trust at runtime.\n2. **Invasive.** `entire` is a per-repo tool. Writing entries to the user's home-level `config.toml` crosses that boundary.\n3. **Codex's intended UX.** The trust gate is a deliberate security mechanism — `/hooks` is the supported flow.\n\nSo the production path stays at: print a one-time hint, user runs `/hooks` once per machine, done.\n\nWe **did** implement the hash + pre-trust dance for the e2e seed (commit `bf3e70f2d`), where it's acceptable because (a) it's test infrastructure with controlled inputs, (b) hash drift fails the e2e tests loudly rather than silently, and (c) `CODEX_HOME` is isolated per test so writes don't leak.\n\nTwo distinct decisions, both intentional:\n- **Production install** → hint, no pre-trust\n- **e2e seed** → pre-trust via replicated hash"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"c