{"parentUuid":"2aada7ab-fcd6-4e88-a710-90921c8f989d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01T94hegNwtsmnrFWeTgX6oa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Banner gone. Now on the re-trust question:\n\n## Why this happens\n\nCodex's `trusted_hash` is a sha256 over the **normalized hook definition** (event + matcher + handler command + timeout + async + statusMessage). So:\n\n- **Adding a new hook** (like our recent PostToolUse) → new entry, no `trusted_hash` for it in `~/.codex/config.toml` → status = `Untrusted` → warning fires\n- **Changing an existing hook's command** (e.g., changing the wrap form, or the `entire hooks codex …` subcommand name) → hash changes → status = `Modified` → warning fires\n- **Removing a hook** → no warning, the orphan trust entry just sits there\n\nSo every time `entire` ships a hook-touching change, every developer's next Codex startup says \"N hooks need review\". That's intrinsic to the trust model — the gate is precisely there so users notice when something changes.\n\n## Can we minimize it\n\nThree knobs we have:\n\n**(1) Stable command shape for existing hooks.** Once a hook ships, never change its wrapper or subcommand name. New entire versions can add hooks but should leave existing ones byte-identical. Trust survives. The only churn is when we genuinely add a new event.\n\n**(2) Bundle hook additions.** If we know multiple hooks are coming (say, PostToolUse + PreCompact + PostCompact), ship them in one entire release so users approve once. Dribbling them across releases means re-approving each time.\n\n**(3) Detect and message at `entire enable`.** This is the brittle path: parse the user's `~/.codex/config.toml`, replicate Codex's hash, compare. We rejected this earlier for production. **But** — if we limited detection to \"did `.codex/hooks.json` change in this enable run?\" (no hash needed, just compare the JSON before/after), we could cheaply tell the setup committer \"you added/changed N hooks; team members will need to re-approve via /hooks after pulling\". That's actually useful — it tells the *one person* who runs `enable` to flag the upgrade in the team channel / commit message.\n\n## Can we authoritatively detect untrusted state per-user\n\nHonestly: **only Codex can.** Its startup warning IS the authoritative answer. We could try to replicate it by parsing `~/.codex/config.toml` and computing hashes (we already do this for the e2e seed), but:\n\n- Hash drift would silently make us under-warn or over-warn  \n- Reading the user's home-level config from `entire enable` crosses the per-repo boundary\n- Codex's existing warning is loud and specific\n\nSo my read: **(1) + (2)** as discipline (cost is just being thoughtful when we touch hooks), and **(3)** if you want concrete in-CLI signal — but only the *setup committer* benefits. Don't replicate Codex's hash for end-user messaging; that path leads back to the brittle dependency we already declined.\n\nA loose fourth option: **a `entire doctor` check** that calls Codex's `hooks/list` JSON-RPC endpoint and surfaces untrusted entries. Heavier — needs an app-server interaction — but gives every developer (not just the committer) a way to ask \"is my codex setup good?\" from the entire CLI. That'd belong in the same follow-up as the e2e preflight framework, since both speak Codex's app-server protocol.\n\nWant me to do (3) — the cheap \"hooks-changed-in-this-enable-run\" detection — or leave it for now?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":424,"cache_read_input_tokens":910966,"output_tokens":1151,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":424,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1151,"cache_read_input_tokens":910966,"cache_creation_input_