{"parentUuid":"8707976b-70dd-4f64-887f-82384fc63c86","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01PJGw2NfxaNXAX89fgmzxNg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes — and worktrees compound the friction. The trust state is in the user's `~/.codex/config.toml`, keyed by the **absolute path of the `.codex/hooks.json` file** as Codex saw it on disk:\n\n```toml\n[hooks.state.\"/Users/you/proj/.codex/hooks.json:session_start:0:0\"]\ntrusted_hash = \"sha256:…\"\n```\n\nThat path is computed via `config_folder.join(\"hooks.json\")` and used as-is — Codex doesn't `canonicalize` it. So:\n\n- Main repo at `/Users/you/proj/` → trust key uses `/Users/you/proj/.codex/hooks.json`\n- Worktree at `/Users/you/proj-feature-x/` → trust key uses `/Users/you/proj-feature-x/.codex/hooks.json`\n- Worktree at `/Users/you/proj-bugfix-y/` → another distinct key\n\nEach worktree is a fresh \"untrusted\" state from Codex's perspective, even though the hooks.json content (and therefore the hash) is identical. The user has to run `/hooks` once per worktree on their machine.\n\nFor users who lean on `git worktree add` heavily (e.g., the `entire` workflow itself, where worktrees are cheap), that's a recurring papercut.\n\n## Workarounds\n\n**Symlink trick won't help.** I checked: Codex uses the path as it saw it (joined from `config_folder`), no canonicalize. If you symlinked `worktree/.codex` → `main/.codex`, Codex would still record the worktree path in the key.\n\n**The realistic options are:**\n\n**(a) Accept it.** One `/hooks` interaction per worktree. Same UX as initial trust; users who use worktrees are usually power users who can absorb the cost.\n\n**(b) Add a `entire codex trust-hooks` (or `entire trust-codex-hooks`) explicit command.** Computes the hash for the current worktree's `.codex/hooks.json` and writes the matching `trusted_hash` entries to the user's `~/.codex/config.toml`. Same brittleness we declined for the auto-install 