{"parentUuid":"cb7f381b-e676-4c7e-8225-e63752f949e9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019BKQmiPkcSVMe8Gek6GnFM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes — and that's a much cleaner check than what we'd been considering. We don't need the hash; we just need **key existence**. Looking at your snippet:\n\n```toml\n[hooks.state.\"<path>:session_start:0:0\"]   ← present\n[hooks.state.\"<path>:stop:0:0\"]            ← present\n[hooks.state.\"<path>:user_prompt_submit:0:0\"] ← present\n                                            ← post_tool_use missing\n```\n\nThat tells us, structurally, that this user has trusted three of our hooks but a fourth one (PostToolUse) needs approval. We don't need to recompute Codex's hash to know this — we're only **reading**, not writing trust.\n\nWhat this catches:\n\n- ✅ **New hook added** (the PostToolUse case in your snippet) — key absent → flag it\n- ✅ **Never trusted anything** — no keys → flag all\n- ✅ **Codex's `~/.codex/config.toml` doesn't exist** — flag all\n- ❌ **Hook command modified** (hash drifted, status = `Modified`) — key still present but hash mismatches; we can't detect this without computing the hash. **Codex's own `⚠ N hooks need review` warning still catches it**, so we don't have to.\n\nSo this is a **partial but useful** signal. The \"key absent\" case is exactly the upgrade scenario you described (we ship a new hook, existing trust survives the others, only the new one needs approval). That's the most common drift over time.\n\n## Where to surface it\n\nThree reasonable spots:\n\n**1. `entire enable --agent codex`** — same surface where the install hint used to live, but now it's actually personalized: only shows when *this user, on this machine* has a missing trust entry. The setup committer sees an accurate \"you'll need to trust 1 new hook\" message; everyone else sees nothing.\n\n**2. `entire doctor`** — generic checkup, runs on demand. Fits the 